re now on average there's one  or two on a given page but it also could  just as easily be a lot more but also  think about if it's Google web fonts  it's going to be cached when you're  loading it from the same CDN all over  the place so you the user might see open  sans on five different sites and you  could conceivable  loading one file so that's a better  experience for you and saves a few bucks  for somebody who was serving the file so  last year three weeks before I came here  at a type I in Warsaw a type and  typography conference the variable font  format was introduced really kind of  blew my mind as a designer and a fan of  good performance on the web seeing what  those implications are and immediately  started thinking about how I might be  able to incorporate that in what I was  talking about thinking about and and  speaking about so I got the demo  literally two nights before I came here  looked at some stuff on stage it was  really fun it didn't actually catch on  fire so I was even better and so this is  getting into looking at see in this case  that is normal page loading 26 files so  this is using s on 600k to get that  whole layout change that over to LaVette  from CJ done it's an experimental font  that he's working on in the variable  format two files that's kind of Awesome  so thinking about all of that being  produced the italic is one file the  Roman is the other that beautiful  display and the initial cap and the body  copy is coming from the same file all  we're doing is changing the weight  changing the width that's it it's really  kind of amazing what you can do with it  and it's all controllable via CSS and  animatable to buy it so that's kind of  awesome we've sort of dealt with our  design language and the expansion of our  vocabulary in addition to dealing with  performance all with this one  improvement so that's really pretty  remarkable and that's not even the whole  story because we can also do things like  in this page where we st